# Every tracked text file is stored and checked out with LF, so what Windows
# packages is byte-identical to what Linux packages:
# `cargo package` reads the working tree, and this machine's
# `core.autocrlf=true` would otherwise hand it CRLF `.rs` sources. The entries
# below opt specific files out (`-text`) or restate this rule where a tool
# reads the bytes. `scripts/check-invariants.py --rules eol` enforces it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings so Git hooks run unchanged on
# Windows, Git Bash, and Linux checkouts regardless of core.autocrlf.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Authoring output fixtures are compared as raw bytes, so Git must never
# normalize or whitespace-check them: RON pretty output legitimately carries
# CRLF, which `git diff --check` would report as trailing whitespace.
cli/tests/fixtures/** -text -diff

# Bevy's vendored font license is compared by SHA-256 against the `v0.19.1` tag
# (`app/assets/fonts/README.md`), so Git must neither normalize nor
# whitespace-check its bytes. The upstream text carries a trailing space.
app/assets/fonts/FiraMono-LICENSE -text -diff

# Generated documentation indexes are compared byte-for-byte against the Git
# index and committed trees, so a checkout must yield the LF bytes the generator
# wrote regardless of core.autocrlf.
docs/INDEX.md text eol=lf
docs/adr/INDEX.md text eol=lf
docs/guide/INDEX.md text eol=lf

# The notices template is rendered into release archives, so a checkout must hand
# cargo-about the same LF bytes on every platform regardless of core.autocrlf.
packaging/third-party-licenses.hbs text eol=lf
